# A choice with the probability x/n of two program variables (#237): n - 1 is geometric, Pr[n = k] = 2^-k for
# k ≥ 1, x is binomial(n, 1/2), and y = 1 with probability x/n. By symmetry Ex[x/n] = 1/2.
#
# Both variables take infinitely many values, so the probability cannot be computed by a case split over them.
# Weighting the coefficient of x^a n^b with a/b is the operator θ_x followed by 1/θ_n (θ_x = x·∂/∂x): the
# branch gets ∫_0^n (θ_x G)(t)/t dt. It is well defined because n > 0 and x ≤ n hold in every state, which
# Prodigy proves by filtering G; the integral is proposed and certified by its derivative and its value at 0.

n: nat = geometric(1/2);
n = n + 1;  # the bound, at least 1
x: nat = iid(bernoulli(1/2), n);  # binomial(n, 1/2), at most n
y: nat = 0;
{
    y = 1;
} [x / n] {
    y = 0;
}
query Pr[y == 1];  # expected: 1/2
query Pr[n == 2 and x == 1 and y == 1];  # expected: 1/4 · 1/2 · 1/2 = 1/16
query Pr[n == 1 and y == 0];  # expected: 1/2 · 1/2 (x = 0) = 1/4
